---
import BaseLayout from '~/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import MetricTile from '~/components/MetricTile.astro';
import { readingTime } from '~/lib/posts';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { PHASES, SEGMENTS, CHANNELS, MARKET, BASELINE } from '~/data/gtm';
import { AUDIENCE } from '~/data/metrics';
import { SITE, AUTHOR } from '~/consts';

// Site facts are computed from the repo rather than typed in, so this page cannot
// drift out of date the way a hand-maintained figure would.
const all = await getCollection('posts');
const arabicWords = all.reduce(
  (sum, p) => sum + (p.body ?? '').replace(/```[\s\S]*?```/g, ' ').split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean).length,
  0,
);
const tagCount = new Set(all.flatMap((p) => p.data.tags)).size;
const minutes = all.reduce((sum, p) => sum + readingTime(p.body), 0);

const fmt = (n: number) => new Intl.NumberFormat('en').format(n);
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`Arabic market plan — ${SITE.name}`}
  description="A 90-day go-to-market plan for SerpApi's Arabic-speaking developer market, with market sizing, target segments and measurement."
  lang="en"
  noindex
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 pt-8 pb-4 sm:pt-14">
    <!-- ---------- Header ---------- -->
    <header class="border-b border-[var(--border)] pb-10">
      <p class="m-0 mb-3 text-xs font-semibold tracking-[0.12em] text-[var(--accent)] uppercase">
        Prepared for SerpApi · Developer Advocate (Arabic)
      </p>
      <h1 class="m-0 mb-4 text-3xl leading-tight font-bold sm:text-4xl">
        A 90-day plan for the Arabic developer market
      </h1>
      <p class="m-0 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-[var(--fg-muted)]">
        You are hiring advocates for eleven languages at once, which tells me this is a systematic
        market-expansion programme rather than a content hire. So instead of describing what I would
        do, I ran the first month's research and published it: SerpApi ranks
        <strong>#1 in Egypt, Saudi and the UAE</strong> for <code>serp api</code>, and is absent
        from the top 20 for every Arabic-language phrasing of the same need — where page one is
        held by machine translation. This page is that data, the 90 days it implies, and how you
        would check whether it worked.
      </p>
      <p class="m-0 mt-4 text-sm text-[var(--fg-subtle)]">
        {AUTHOR.name} · <a href={`mailto:${AUTHOR.email}`} class="text-[var(--accent)] no-underline">{AUTHOR.email}</a>
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose text-base">
      <!-- ---------- Opportunity ---------- -->
      <h2>The gap, in four numbers</h2>
      <p>
        The Arabic market is not an emerging one that needs educating. It is a connected, funded
        software market whose developers are underserved in their own language.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="my-8 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
      {MARKET.map((fact) => (
        <div class="rounded-xl border border-[var(--border)] bg-[var(--bg-raised)] p-5">
          <p class="m-0 text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--accent)] tabular-nums">{fact.figure}</p>
          <p class="m-0 mt-1 text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--fg)]">{fact.label}</p>
          <p class="m-0 mt-2 text-[13px] leading-relaxed text-[var(--fg-muted)]">{fact.note}</p>
          <a
            href={fact.source.url}
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="mt-1 inline-block py-1 text-[13px] text-[var(--fg-subtle)] underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-[var(--accent)]"
          >{fact.source.label}</a>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

    <div class="prose text-base">
      <p>
        Note the second figure honestly: sources disagree by a factor of five on how much of the web
        is in Arabic. I would rather show you that disagreement than pick the number that makes the
        strongest slide. The direction is not in dispute, and in technical content specifically the
        gap is wider than any of these aggregate figures suggest.
      </p>

      <!-- ---------- Findings ---------- -->
      <h2>What I already measured</h2>
      <p>
        Rather than describe what I would do, I ran the first month's research and published it.
        Everything below was measured on {BASELINE.measuredOn} through SerpApi, cost
        {BASELINE.searchesUsed} searches, and is reproducible from two open-source tools whose raw
        output is committed alongside them. Check the numbers rather than take them.
      </p>

      <h3>Finding 1 — English demand is captured. Arabic demand is not.</h3>
      <p>
        I tracked <code>serpapi.com</code> across <code>google.com.eg</code>,
        <code>google.com.sa</code> and <code>google.ae</code>, on the same concept expressed two
        ways: in English technical terms, and in Arabic.
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Keyword</th><th>EG</th><th>SA</th><th>AE</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {BASELINE.ranks.map((r) => (
              <tr>
                <td dir={r.script === 'arabic' ? 'rtl' : 'ltr'} class="whitespace-nowrap">{r.keyword}</td>
                {(['eg', 'sa', 'ae'] as const).map((code) => (
                  <td class:list={['text-center font-mono', r[code] === null ? 'text-[var(--fg-subtle)]' : 'font-bold text-[var(--accent)]']}>
                    {r[code] ?? '—'}
                  </td>
                ))}
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="text-[13px] text-[var(--fg-subtle)]">— means absent from the top 20.</p>
      <p>
        SerpApi holds <strong>position 1 in all three markets</strong> for <code>serp api</code>,
        and top-3 for <code>google search api</code>. That is a strong result and it is worth
        saying so. But on every Arabic-language phrasing of the same need, it does not appear at
        all. Those are two different markets, and only one of them is being served.
      </p>

      <h3>Finding 2 — page one is occupied by Google Translate</h3>
      <p>
        Absence proves little on its own, so I looked at what <em>does</em> rank. On
        <code dir="rtl">واجهة برمجة نتائج البحث</code> from <code>google.com.sa</code>,
        <strong><code>translate.google.com</code> holds six of the eight page-one slots.</strong>
        On the next query, three of eight.
      </p>
      <p>
        Google is serving machine-translated English pages because there is no native Arabic
        content worth ranking. One of those translated results is a "which SERP API should you
        choose" comparison article — commercial-intent content, ranking on page one of Arabic
        search, via automatic translation.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is the finding I would build the whole strategy on. The Arabic first page for this
        category is not a crowded market to break into. <strong>It is vacant, and the incumbent is
        a translation engine.</strong>
      </p>

      <h3>Finding 3 — Arabic search fragments, but only for local intent</h3>
      <p>
        I expected Arabic results to differ sharply between countries. Measuring it properly —
        Jaccard overlap of the top-10 domain sets per market — shows the truth is conditional, and
        more useful than the blanket claim would have been.
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Query</th><th>Intent type</th><th>Overlap</th><th>Shared by all 3</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {BASELINE.overlap.map((o) => (
              <tr>
                <td dir="rtl" class="whitespace-nowrap">{o.keyword}</td>
                <td class="text-[var(--fg-muted)] whitespace-nowrap">{o.intent}</td>
                <td class="text-center font-mono font-bold">{o.mean.toFixed(2)}</td>
                <td class="text-center font-mono text-[var(--fg-muted)]">{o.shared}/10</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Overlap tracks how local the intent is, on a clean gradient. Educational queries are
        effectively one pan-Arab market. Local commercial services share <strong>literally zero
        domains</strong> across Egypt, Saudi and the UAE — ten Egyptian agencies, ten Saudi, ten
        Emirati, no name repeating. Add <code>serp api</code> ranking identically in all three and
        the range runs from total convergence to total separation, explained by one variable.
      </p>
      <p>
        The mean across all five is 0.23, and I would not quote that number: it hides the entire
        structure. The commercial consequence is concrete —
        <strong>for commercial-intent Arabic keywords, one rank number is measuring nothing</strong>,
        and the correct unit is a matrix. That is unserved demand that SerpApi's API already
        satisfies, and it multiplies queries per account: five markets, five times the searches for
        the same keyword list.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[15px] text-[var(--fg-subtle)]">
        Honest limits: five keywords, three markets, one point in time, chosen to test a
        hypothesis. Search results move. This is a baseline worth extending, not a study.
      </p>

      <!-- ---------- Plan ---------- -->
      <h2>The 90 days</h2>
    </div>

    <ol class="m-0 my-8 list-none space-y-4 p-0">
      {PHASES.map((phase) => (
        <li class="rounded-xl border border-[var(--border)] bg-[var(--bg-raised)] p-6">
          <div class="mb-3 flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-3">
            <span class="rounded-md bg-[var(--accent-soft)] px-2 py-0.5 font-mono text-[13px] font-semibold text-[var(--accent)]">
              {phase.window}
            </span>
            <h3 class="m-0 text-lg font-bold">{phase.title}</h3>
          </div>
          <p class="m-0 mb-4 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[var(--fg-muted)]">{phase.goal}</p>
          <ul class="m-0 mb-4 list-none space-y-2 p-0">
            {phase.deliverables.map((d) => (
              <li class="flex gap-2.5 text-[15px] leading-relaxed">
                {d.done ? (
                  <svg class="mt-[5px] size-4 shrink-0 text-[var(--accent)]" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.4" aria-hidden="true">
                    <path d="m4 10.5 4 4 8-9" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" />
                  </svg>
                ) : (
                  <span class="mt-[9px] size-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-[var(--border-strong)]" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                )}
                <span>
                  {d.text}
                  {d.done && <span class="ms-2 align-middle text-[12px] font-semibold tracking-wide text-[var(--accent)] uppercase">Done</span>}
                </span>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
          <p class="m-0 border-t border-[var(--border)] pt-3 text-[13px] text-[var(--fg-subtle)]">
            <span class="font-semibold text-[var(--fg-muted)]">Proof it worked:</span> {phase.proof}
          </p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>

    <!-- ---------- Segments ---------- -->
    <div class="prose text-base">
      <h2>Who buys SERP data in this region</h2>
      <p>
        These are hypotheses, not conclusions. Which segment actually converts is an empirical
        question, and part of what the first 30 days is for.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="my-8 space-y-3">
      {SEGMENTS.map((segment, i) => (
        <div class="rounded-xl border border-[var(--border)] p-5">
          <div class="flex items-baseline gap-3">
            <span class="font-mono text-[13px] text-[var(--fg-subtle)] tabular-nums">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
            <h3 class="m-0 text-base font-bold">{segment.name}</h3>
          </div>
          <p class="m-0 mt-2 ps-8 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[var(--fg-muted)]">{segment.why}</p>
          <ul class="m-0 mt-3 ps-8 flex list-none flex-wrap gap-2 p-0">
            {segment.examples.map((e) => (
              <li class="rounded-md border border-[var(--border)] bg-[var(--bg-sunken)] px-2 py-0.5 text-[13px] text-[var(--fg-muted)]">{e}</li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

    <!-- ---------- Channels ---------- -->
    <div class="prose text-base">
      <h2>Channels, and what each is for</h2>
      <p>
        The job description asks for blogs, videos, social, community and outbound. A blog alone
        covers one of five, so here is how I would weight the rest.
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Channel</th><th>Role</th><th>Cadence</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {CHANNELS.map((c) => (
              <tr>
                <td class="font-semibold whitespace-nowrap">{c.name}</td>
                <td>{c.role}</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap text-[var(--fg-muted)]">{c.cadence}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <!-- ---------- Measurement ---------- -->
      <h2>How you would know it is working</h2>
      <p>
        Developer advocacy earns a reputation for being unmeasurable, usually because the metrics get
        chosen after the fact. These are the ones I would commit to up front.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="my-8 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-3">
      <MetricTile
        value={AUDIENCE.readers30d !== null ? fmt(AUDIENCE.readers30d) : null}
        label="Readers / 30 days"
        note="Leading indicator. Slow to move, then compounds."
      />
      <MetricTile
        value={AUDIENCE.subscribers !== null ? fmt(AUDIENCE.subscribers) : null}
        label="Newsletter subscribers"
        note="Intent, not traffic. The number that predicts signups."
      />
      <MetricTile
        value={AUDIENCE.topOrganicQuery}
        label="Top organic Arabic query"
        note="Whether the keyword strategy actually ranks."
      />
    </div>

    <div class="prose text-base">
      <p class="text-[15px] text-[var(--fg-subtle)]">
        {AUDIENCE.asOf
          ? `Audience figures as of ${AUDIENCE.asOf}.`
          : 'Audience figures are blank because this site is new and I would rather show you an empty field than a number I cannot stand behind. They populate from analytics as the content ages.'}
      </p>
      <p>
        Beyond those three, the metrics that would actually decide whether the hire paid off:
        <strong>signups attributable to Arabic-language channels</strong>, queries per account from
        the region, and — the one most advocates skip — how many product changes came out of regional
        user feedback. That last one is the difference between a content channel and an advocate.
      </p>

      <!-- ---------- Evidence ---------- -->
      <h2>What already exists</h2>
      <p>
        This site is the working prototype of the content system described above, not a mockup of one.
        The figures below are computed from the repository at build time.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="my-8 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4">
      <MetricTile value={fmt(all.length)} label="Arabic posts written" />
      <MetricTile value={fmt(arabicWords)} label="Words of Arabic technical prose" />
      <MetricTile value={fmt(minutes)} label="Minutes of reading" />
      <MetricTile value={fmt(tagCount)} label="Topics covered" />
    </div>

    <div class="prose text-base">
      <p>Specific things it demonstrates, each of which was a decision rather than a default:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Two open-source measurement tools</strong>, with their raw output committed —
          a per-market rank tracker and a SERP-overlap comparator. Every figure on this page is
          reproducible from them.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Correct handling of a live API change.</strong> Google discontinued the
          <code>num</code> parameter in 2025, so tooling that still requests 100 results silently
          sees ten and reports false negatives. Both tools paginate with <code>start</code> and
          compute absolute rank from the page offset.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Arabic typography and bidi handled properly.</strong> Code blocks and Latin
          technical terms are directionally isolated, so a URL or an identifier inside Arabic prose
          does not get reordered. Most Arabic technical sites get this wrong.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Multi-language code samples</strong> in Python, JavaScript and cURL, matching how
          SerpApi's own docs present snippets.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Open Graph images that render Arabic correctly</strong> — which required rejecting
          the standard tool for the job, because it cannot shape Arabic script.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>An English abstract on every post</strong>, enforced by the content schema. Your
          team cannot read Arabic, and content you cannot evaluate is content you cannot manage.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The last point is the one I would emphasise. Managing an Arabic content programme from Austin
        requires the output to be legible to people who do not read the language. That is a
        solvable problem, and it should be solved on day one rather than becoming a trust gap later.
      </p>

      <h2>What I would want to know from you</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Which regional Google domains already show meaningful query volume in your logs — that would sharpen the market prioritisation immediately.</li>
        <li>Whether Arabic localisation is expected to cover the docs and dashboard, or marketing pages only.</li>
        <li>How the other language advocates coordinate, and what is shared versus owned per market.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="my-12 rounded-2xl border border-[var(--border)] bg-[var(--bg-sunken)] p-6 sm:p-8">
      <p class="m-0 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[var(--fg-muted)]">
        The Arabic posts referenced above are at
        <a href="/" class="font-semibold text-[var(--accent)] no-underline">{SITE.url.replace('https://', '')}</a>,
        with English summaries on every post and a full English index at
        <a href="/en" class="font-semibold text-[var(--accent)] no-underline">/en</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="m-0 mt-4 text-[15px]">
        <a href={`mailto:${AUTHOR.email}`} class="font-semibold text-[var(--accent)] no-underline">{AUTHOR.email}</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
